## 4. ATTEMPTS to enlarge vm_space — NOT shipped (all broke boot)

Each attempt below was implemented, rebuilt, and QEMU-booted. None
booted cleanly, so ALL were reverted; the shipped tree is the proven
main-CSpace-only fix (§1). The failures are recorded so the next
iteration knows what does NOT work and why.

  ATTEMPT A — NUM_CNODE_3RD_LOG2 3 -> 6 (8 -> 64 third-level groups),
               eager vm constructor unchanged.
    NUM_VM_SEL_LOG2 = 7+5+6 = 18 (262144). BUT the `_cnodes[64]` array
    inside each Platform_pd grew to ~84 KiB; core's heap (which retains
    only ~total-16Kpool-kernel RAM after assigning the rest to init)
    could not allocate init's Platform_pd -> core's PD-session factory
    retried 10x and gave up:
      "Warning: giving up to increase session quota for PD session
       after 10 attempts / Error: PD session (label="init",
       ram_quota=233472, cap_quota=13) unavailable"
    (logs p4-falkon-cspace-fix-run2/3.log). Confirmed it was the array
    size, not the lazy mechanism, because making the constructor lazy
    (Attempt B) did not help while NUM_CNODE_3RD stayed 64.

  ATTEMPT B — lazy vm_space leaf construction (_ensure_leaf constructs
               3rd-level + leaf on first map; _core_cnode member added;
               constructed() reduced to the vm_pad root; Construct_cnode
               ::destruct made no-op-safe) + NUM_CNODE_3RD_LOG2=6.
    Still failed with the SAME "PD session unavailable" (run4/5): the
    `_cnodes[64]` ARRAY (84 KiB) is part of the Platform_pd object
    regardless of lazy construction, so core's heap was still starved.

  ATTEMPT C — LEAF_CNODE_SIZE_LOG2 7 -> 9 (512-entry, 16 KiB-backed
               leaves) + NUM_CNODE_3RD_LOG2 3 -> 4 (16 groups):
               NUM_VM_SEL_LOG2 = 9+5+4 = 18, small `_cnodes[16]` array
               (~22 KiB, no heap starvation), lazy construction.
    FAILED earlier in boot — core produced NO serial output (died
    during Platform() construction, before "Genode 26.05"):
      (logs p4-falkon-cspace-fix-run8/9.log: build completes, QEMU
       starts, no output, run_genode_until times out, error 143).

  ATTEMPT D — bisect: vm_space reverted to UPSTREAM but the x86_64 16K
               pool sizing left sized for vm leaves (incoherent).
    Booted to "Genode 26.05" then died at child-PD construction with
    the seL4 kernel diagnostic:
      "<<seL4: Untyped Retype: Creating kernel objects with device
       untyped>> / seL4_Untyped_RetypeAtOffset (cnode) returned 1 /
       Cnode construction failed / null cap #4194304 / failed to create
       initial thread for new PD"
    (captured via direct QEMU on the bisect image). This is the size-
    aware Cnode ctor drawing a bad 16K untyped while the pool sizing was
    incoherent with the (reverted) vm geometry.

  COMMON THREAD: every enlargement that reached NUM_VM_SEL_LOG2=18
  broke core boot, via one of: (a) Platform_pd `_cnodes` array starving
  core heap; (b) core dying silently during Platform() with the larger
  vm_space object; (c) the 16K untyped pool handing out a bad cap when
  the pool sizing and vm geometry disagreed. The 16K allocation path
  itself is sound (it is the proven VCPU path), so the failure is in
  the INTERACTION between (vm_space geometry, Platform_pd object size,
  max_page_frames() driving core's virt reservation at platform.cc:650,
  and the 16K pool reservation), not in any single file.

  RECOMMENDED NEXT STEP (for the orchestrator / next iteration):
    - Keep the shipped main-CSpace fix.
    - Enlarge vm_space with LAZY leaf construction (so core's vm stays
      small) AND a small `_cnodes` array: use LEAF_CNODE_SIZE_LOG2=9 +
      NUM_CNODE_3RD_LOG2=4 (NUM_VM_SEL_LOG2=18, `_cnodes[16]` ~22 KiB).
    - Reconcile `platform.cc:650`'s `2 * max_page_frames() * PAGE_SIZE`
      (core's dynamic virt reservation) — at NUM_VM_SEL_LOG2=18 it
      tries to reserve 2 GiB and pre-allocate page tables for it during
      Platform(); CAP it (e.g. min(..., 256 MiB)).
    - Size the 16K pool for BOTH the main CSpace AND the vm leaves
      (each PD: (1+2^1ST) main CNodes + NUM_CNODE_3RD*NUM_LEAF_CNODES
      vm leaves, each 16 KiB) and verify the pool carving never hands
      out a device/uninitialised 16K untyped (the seL4 diagnostic in
      Attempt D). The pool population callback already skips device
      ranges; the residual bad-cap case needs a seL4-cap-level trace.
    - Each sub-change is independently testable; the failing
      interactions above are the map.

